ust have at least {min_length} characters.\",\n        \"pattern\": \"Must match pattern {pattern}.\",\n        \"format\": \"Must be a valid {format}.\",\n    }\n```\n\nYes, I'm quite confident. The message would be \"Invalid argument 'name' for Example(). Must have at most 10 characters.\"\n\nBut to be safe against version differences, I'll use a substring assertion: `\"Invalid argument 'name' for Example().\" in message` and also check that the error text portion is present with \"at most 10 characters\" (without the trailing period to be extra safe? No \u2014 the format string includes the period). Hmm. Let me just assert:\n\n```python\nassert message.startswith(\"Invalid argument 'name' for Example().\")\n```\n\nand additionally assert the error text is included:\n\n```python\nassert \"Must have at most 10 characters.\" in message\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident this is right for typesystem. If the version differs slightly, the test could fail... but the task says \"make sure that each test is correct\". Given the get_info output shows `Field.__init__` with `NO_DEFAULT` sentinel and `_creation_counter`, this matches typesystem 0.4.x. In typesystem 0.4.x, String errors are as I recalled. Let me double check typesystem 0.4.1 fields.py from memory:\n\n```python\nclass String(Field):\n    errors = {\n        \"type\": \"Must be a string.\",\n        \"null\": \"May not be null.\",\n        \"blank\": \"Must not be blank.\",\n        \"max_length\": \"Must have at most {max_length} characters.\",\n        \"min_length\": \"Must have at least {min_length} characters.\",\n        \"pattern\": \"Must match pattern {pattern}.\",\n        \"format\": \"Must be a valid {format}.\",\n    }\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        *,\n        min_length: int = None,\n        max_length: int = None,\n        pattern: str = None,\n        format: str = None,\n        trim_whitespace: bool = True,\n        **kwargs: typing.Any,\n    ) -> None:\n        ...\n```\n\nYes. And va