I verify `is_sparse` or Mapping behavior? Not needed.\n\nNow, module-level code: imports, one fixture, test functions. That's minimal and necessary.\n\nShould I guard against `typesystem` import differences? `import typesystem` gives `typesystem.Schema`, `typesystem.String`, `typesystem.Integer` \u2014 all public API. Good.\n\nOne more potential concern: the fixture defines the class each time \u2014 the metaclass sorts fields by `_creation_counter`, fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 hmm, wait. There's one subtle thing about `SchemaMetaclass.__new__` signature: it accepts `definitions` kwarg. Defining a class normally doesn't pass it. Fine.\n\nLet me also think about whether `assert not kwargs` in line 98 \u2014 in test 11, `Example({}, name=\"foo\")` \u2014 args=({},), kwargs={\"name\": \"foo\"} \u2192 line 97 passes, line 98 fails \u2192 AssertionError. Good.\n\nNow write the final file. Also add a docstring? Not necessary. Keep it clean.\n\nAlso, per instructions: \"Be sure to include assertions in the test that verify any applicable postconditions.\" \u2014 done via attribute assertions and message assertions.\n\n\"Please also make VERY SURE to clean up after the test, so as to avoid state pollution\" \u2014 classes defined inside fixture/test functions; no globals modified; no monkeypatch needed. I could add a comment noting this. Maybe a brief comment in the fixture: \"Schema classes are created inside the fixture so no module-level state is polluted.\" That's fine.\n\nFinal code:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nimport typesystem\n\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef example_schema():\n    # Schema classes are created inside the fixture (rather than at module\n    # level) so that no global state is created or polluted by the tests.\n    class Example(typesystem.Schema):\n        name = typesystem.String(max_length=10)\n        age = typesystem.Integer(default=0)\n\n    return Example\n\n\ndef test_init_with_dict_sets_present_fields(example_schema):\n    instance = example_schema({\"na