self.value\n        yield self.error\n```\n\nor returns iter(tuple). Since the code under test unpacks it, it must work. Good.\n\nAnd `error.messages()` \u2014 `ValidationError` from typesystem.base has `messages()` returning list of `Message` with `.text`. The code under test uses it, so it exists.\n\nNow, about `ValidationError` for a simple field error: when `String.validate` raises `self.error(\"max_length\")`, that creates a `Message`, and... hmm, actually how does a `Message` become a `ValidationError`? In typesystem, `Field.error` returns a `Message`? And `validate` raises... Let me think. In typesystem base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text, code=None, index=(), ...):\n        ...\n\nclass ValidationError(Message):\n    def __init__(self, *, text, code=None, index=(), ...):\n        ...\n    def messages(self, ...):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually in typesystem, `ValidationError` extends `Message` and `messages()` returns `[self]` for simple errors. So `error.messages()` \u2192 `[error]`, and `error.text` is the text. So `error_text` = \"Must have at most 10 characters.\" Good.\n\nActually wait, in newer typesystem, `Field.error` might return `ValidationError` directly:\n\n```python\n    def error(self, code: str) -> Message:\n        text = self.errors[code].format(**self.__dict__)\n        return Message(text=text, code=code)\n```\n\nand validate raises it... but `validate_or_error` catches `ValidationError`. Hmm, if `self.error` returns a `Message` (not ValidationError), then raising it wouldn't be caught... So it must be that `Message` and `ValidationError`... Let me recall typesystem/base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text: str, code: str = None, index: typing.Tuple = ()) -> None:\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.index = index\n\nclass ValidationError(Message):\n    def __init__(self, *, text: str, code: str = None, index=(), error=...):\n        ...\n    def messages(self, *, prefix_key=\"\") -> typing.List[Message]:\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, and in fields.py:\n\n```python\n    def error(self, code: str) -> Message:\n        text = self.errors[code].format(**self.__dict__)\n        return Message(text=text, code=code)\n```\n\nHmm wait, that returns Message, but validate raises it and validate_or_error catches ValidationError... That would be a bug. Let me reconsider \u2014 maybe `Field.error` returns `ValidationError`:\n\nActually from typesystem source (I recall now):\n\n```python\nclass Field:\n    ...\n    def error(self, code: str) -> Message:\n        text = self.errors[code].format(**self.__dict__)\n        return Message(text=text, code=code)\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me think about typesystem's actual base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text: str, code: typing.Any = None, index: typing.Tuple[typing.Union[str, int], ...] = ()) -> None:\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.index = index\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        if isinstance(other, Message):\n            return self.text == other.text and self.code == other.code and self.index == other.index\n        else:\n            return self.text == other\n\n    def __hash__(self) -> int:\n        return hash((self.text, self.code))\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return self.text\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return f\"{self.__class__.__name__}({self.text!r})\"\n\n\nclass ValidationError(Message):\n    @property\n    def messages_dict(self) ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. But regardless \u2014 the code under test calls `error.messages()` and joins `message.text`. For a simple field validation failure, this yields the field's error text. This is well-established typesystem behavior (e.g., `Schema.validate_or_error` produces similar messages). I'm confident the joined text for a single max_length error is \"Must have at most 10 characters.\"\n\nOK, I'm c