()` with a non-numeric string \u2192 \"Must be a number.\" Hmm, also from memory. Or use `allow_null=False` and pass None \u2192 \"May not be null.\"? Also memory-based. All error texts are memory-based since get_info couldn't find String/Integer. The startswith assertion on the code-under-test's own format string is fully determined by the given code, so that's guaranteed. The contains part is the only memory-dependent bit. I'll keep it but make it a substring that's very likely stable: \"at most 10 characters\". Hmm, if the format were \"Must have at most 10 character.\" (singular bug)... unlikely. I'll use \"Must have at most 10 characters.\" \u2014 standard.\n\nActually, you know what \u2014 to be maximally safe while still verifying the postcondition (TypeError raised with a message identifying the field and class), I could assert:\n\n```python\nmessage = str(excinfo.value)\nassert message.startswith(\"Invalid argument 'name' for Example().\")\nassert message.endswith(\"Must have at most 10 characters.\")\n```\n\nThat's fine. Or just contains. I'll do:\n\n```python\nassert \"Invalid argument 'name' for Example().\" in message\nassert \"Must have at most 10 characters.\" in message\n```\n\nOK.\n\nNow, cleanup/state pollution: \n- Schema classes defined inside fixtures/tests \u2192 no module-level pollution.\n- `Field._creation_counter` is a global counter incremented on each Field creation \u2014 that's inherent to the library, not something we need to clean up.\n- No monkeypatching needed since we don't modify any global state.\n\nThe instruction says \"use 'monkeypatch' or 'pytest-mock' if appropriate\" \u2014 not needed here; defining classes locally in a fixture is the isolation mechanism. I'll note that implicitly by using a fixture.\n\nNow, one concern: defining the schema class inside a fixture \u2014 the fixture returns the class; tests instantiate it. Each test gets a fresh class. Good.\n\nLet me also double check `test_init_with_dict_skips_absent_fields`: `Example({\"name\": \"foo\"})` \u2014 dict branch: for key in fields (name, age): name in item \u2192 setattr name=\"foo\". age not in item \u2192 skip. return. So instance.name == \"foo\", instance has no `age` attribute. Assert `not hasattr(instance, \"age\")`. Good \u2014 this covers the `key in item` False path.\n\nAnd `test_init_with_dict_ignores_unknown_keys`: `Example({\"name\": \"foo\", \"unknown\": 1})` \u2014 loop only over fields, so \"unknown\" never visited. instance.name set, no `unknown` attribute. This is a nice postcondition test. It doesn't add branch coverage beyond test 2 but verifies behavior. Keep it? It's fine, adds correctness verification. I'll keep it but maybe merge with test 2. Let me merge: `Example({\"name\": \"foo\", \"unknown\": 1})` covers both absent field (age) and unknown key. Actually merging reduces test count; but separate tests are clearer. I'll keep them separate-ish... Actually let me merge to keep the file tight: one dict test with all keys, one dict test with partial + extra keys.\n\nLet me restructure:\n\n```python\ndef test_init_with_dict_sets_fields_and_ignores_others(example_schema):\n    instance = example_schema({\"name\": \"foo\", \"age\": 3})\n    assert instance.name == \"foo\"\n    assert instance.age == 3\n\ndef test_init_with_dict_skips_missing_and_unknown_keys(example_schema):\n    instance = example_schema({\"name\": \"foo\", \"unknown\": \"ignored\"})\n    assert instance.name == \"foo\"\n    assert not hasattr(instance, \"age\")\n    assert not hasattr(instance, \"unknown\")\n```\n\nGood.\n\nObject tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_init_with_object_copies_matching_attributes(example_schema):\n    class Person:\n        name = \"bar\"\n        age = 21\n\n    instance = example_schema(Person())\n    assert instance.name == \"bar\"\n    assert instance.age == 21\n\ndef test_init_with_object_skips_missing_attributes(example_schema):\n    class Person:\n        name = \"bar\"\n\n    instance = example_schema(Person())\n    assert instance.name == \"bar\"\n    assert not hasattr(instance, \"age\")\n```\n\nKwargs tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_init_with_kwargs_validates_and_sets_fields(example_schema):\n    instance = example_schema(name=\"foo\", age=3)\n    assert instance.name == \"foo\"\n    assert instance.age == 3\n\ndef test_init_applies_field_defaults(example_schema):\n    instance = example_schema(name=\"foo\")\n    assert instance.name == \"foo\"\n    assert instance.age == 0\n\ndef test_init_leaves_field_unset_when_no_default():\n    class Example(typesystem.Schema):\n        name = typesystem.String()\n        age = typesystem.Integer()\n\n    instance = Example(name=\"foo\")\n    assert instance.name == \"foo\"\n    with pytest.raises(AttributeError):\n        instance.age\n```\n\nError tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_init_invalid_kwarg_value_raises_type_error(example_schema):\n    with pytest.raises(TypeError) as excinfo:\n        example_schema(name=\"x\" * 11)\n    message = str(excinfo.value)\n    assert \"Invalid argument 'name' for Example().\" in message\n    assert \"Must have at most 10 characters.\" in message\n\ndef test_init_unknown_kwarg_raises_type_error(example_schema):\n    with pytest.raises(TypeError) as excinfo:\n        example_schema(name=\"foo\", unknown=1)\n    assert str(excinfo.value) == \"'unknown' is an invalid keyword argument for Example().\"\n```\n\nAssertion tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_init_with_multiple_positional_args_raises_assertion_error(example_schema):\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError):\n        example_schema({}, {})\n\ndef test_init_with_positional_arg_and_kwargs_raises_assertion_error(example_schema):\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError):\n        example_schema({}, name=\"foo\")\n```\n\nWait \u2014 careful with `example_schema({}, {})`: `assert len(args) == 1` fails first. Good. `example_schema({}, name=\"foo\")`: len(args)==1 passes, `assert not kwargs` fails. Good.\n\nHmm, one thing about the fixture: `example_schema` returns the class; calling `example_schema({...})` in tests \u2014 the fixture is a function returning a class, so `example_schema({...})` calls the class. That works since the fixture value IS the class. \n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtle pytest gotcha: fixture named `example_schema` used as `example_schema(...)` inside test \u2014 yes, the test parameter is the class object, calling it constructs an instance. Fine.\n\nAlso, should the fixture use `yield` for cleanup? Nothing to clean up. Plain return.\n\nOne more consideration: `typesystem.String(max_length=10)` \u2014 does String accept `max_length` kwarg? Yes, standard typesystem API. And `typesystem.Integer(default=0)` \u2014 yes.\n\nAlso `typesystem.String()` with no args \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, is there any issue with `Schema` being a `Mapping` and `instance.name` \u2014 no.\n\nAlso, potential issue: `Schema.__eq__` \u2014 not relevant.\n\nEdge: In `test_init_leaves_field_unset_when_no_default`, after `Example(name=\"foo\")`, `instance.age` raises AttributeError. But wait \u2014 could `Mapping` provide `age`? No. Could `Schema` class have `fields` only. Good.\n\nHmm, one more: in the metaclass, `attrs['fields'] = dict(sorted(fields.items(), key=lambda item: item[1]._creation_counter))`. So `self.fields` iteration order is creation order: name, age. Fine.\n\nAlso \u2014 `Field._creation_counter` \u2014 in `Field.__init__`: `self._creation_counter = Field._creation_counter; Field._creation_counter += 1`. So there's a class attribute `_creation_counter` on Field initialized somewhere (e.g., `_creation_counter = 0` at class level... actually it's probably `itertools.count()` or an int). Not our concern.\n\nNow \u2014 one thing I want to double-check: `validate_or_error` returns `ValidationResult`, and the code does `value, error = schema.validate_or_error(value)`. `ValidationResult.__iter__` is defined (body elided). Presumably:\n\n```python\n    def __iter__(self) -> typing.Iterator:\n        yield self.value\n        yield self.error\n```\n\nor returns iter(tuple). Since the code under test unpacks it, it must work. Good.\n\nAnd `error.messages()` \u2014 `ValidationError` from typesystem.base has `messages()` returning list of `Message` with `.text`. The code under test uses it, so it exists.\n\nNow, about `ValidationError` for a simple field error: when `String.validate` raises `self.error(\"max_length\")`, that creates a `Message`, and... hmm, actually how does a `Message` become a `ValidationError`? In typesystem, `Field.error` returns a `Message`? And `validate` raises... Let me think. In typesystem base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text, code=None, index=(), ...):\n        ...\n\nclass ValidationError(Message):\n    def __init__(self, *, text, code=None, index=(), ...):\n        ...\n    def messages(self, ...):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually in typesystem, `ValidationError` extends `Message` and `messages()` returns `[self]` for simple errors. So `error.messages()` \u2192 `[error]`, and `error.text` is the text. So `error_text` = \"Must have at most 10 characters.\" Good.\n\nActually wait, in newer typesystem, `Field.error` might return `ValidationError` directly:\n\n```python\n    def error(self, code: str) -> Message:\n        text = self.errors[code].format(**self.__dict__)\n        return Message(text=text, code=code)\n```\n\nand validate raises it... but `validate_or_error` catches `ValidationError`. Hmm, if `self.error` returns a `Message` (not ValidationError), then raising it wouldn't be caught... So it must be that `Message` and `ValidationError`... Let me recall typesystem/base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text: str, code: str = None, index: typing.Tuple = ()) -> None:\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.index = index\n\nclass ValidationError(Message):\n    def __init__(self, *, text: str, code: str = None, index=(), error=...):\n        ...\n    def messages(self, *, prefix_key=\"\") -> typing.List[Message]:\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, and in fields.py:\n\n```python\n    def error(self, code: str) -> Message:\n        text = self.errors[code].format(**self.__dict__)\n        return Message(text=text, code=code)\n```\n\nHmm wait, that returns Message, but validate raises it and validate_or_error catches ValidationError... That would be a bug. Let me reconsider \u2014 maybe `Field.error` returns `ValidationError`:\n\nActually from typesystem source (I recall now):\n\n```python\nclass Field:\n    ...\n    def error(self, code: str) -> Message:\n        text = self.errors[code].format(**self.__dict__)\n        return Message(text=text, code=code)\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me think about typesystem's actual base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text: str, code: typing.Any = None, index: typing.Tuple[typing.Union[str, int], ...] = ()) -> None:\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.index = index\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        if isinstance(other, Message):\n            return self.text == other.text and self.code == other.code and self.index == other.index\n        else:\n            return self.text == other\n\n    def __hash__(self) -> int:\n        return hash((self.text, self.code))\n\n    def __str__(self) -> str:\n        return self.text\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return f\"{self.__class__.__name__}({self.text!r})\"\n\n\nclass ValidationError(Message):\n    @property\n    def messages_dict(self) ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. But regardless \u2014 the code under test calls `error.messages()` and joins `message.text`. For a simple field validation failure, this yields the field's error text. This is well-established typesystem behavior (e.g., `Schema.validate_or_error` produces similar messages). I'm confident the joined text for a single max_length error is \"Must have at most 10 characters.\"\n\nOK, I'm confident enough. Let me also consider using `pytest.raises(TypeError, match=...)` \u2014 `match` uses re.search, and the message contains regex-special chars like `.` and `()` and `'`. I'd need escaping. Using excinfo and plain string ops is cleaner.\n\nNow, let me also think: should I add a test where the FIRST field errors and there are also remaining kwargs? Not needed.\n\nWhat about a test where kwargs contains a field key AND an unknown key, ensuring the unknown-key TypeError happens after processing? That's test 9. Good.\n\nWhat about `if kwargs:` where kwargs has ONLY unknown keys (no valid field keys)? E.g., `Example(unknown=1)` \u2014 loop: name not in kwargs, has_default \u2192 set name default? Wait, name = String(max_length=10) has no default \u2192 skip. age not in kwargs \u2192 default 0 set. Then kwargs {\"unknown\": 1} \u2192 TypeError. This covers line 124 False for `name` too. Hmm, my test 7 (`no_default` class) covers 124-False for age. Test 9 with the fixture covers 124-False for name (String has no default) and 124-True for age. So both branches of 124 are covered across tests. Good.\n\nLet me also double check test 9's flow with the fixture: fields = {name: String(max_length=10), age: Integer(default=0)}. `Example(name=\"foo\", unknown=1)`: \n- key=name: in kwargs \u2192 pop \"foo\" \u2192 validate ok \u2192 setattr name=\"foo\".\n- key=age: not in kwargs \u2192 has_default True \u2192 setattr age=0.\n- kwargs = {\"unknown\": 1} \u2192 truthy \u2192 TypeError \"'unknown' is an invalid keyword argument for Example().\"\n\nGood.\n\nAnd test 8: `Example(name=\"x\"*11)`:\n- key=name: in kwargs \u2192 pop \u2192 validate_or_error \u2192 error \u2192 raise TypeError(\"Invalid argument 'name' for Example(). Must have at most 10 characters.\")\nGood.\n\nNow, one more consideration on the dict test: `Example({\"name\": \"foo\", \"age\": 3})` \u2014 dict branch sets attributes directly without validation. instance.age == 3 (int). Good.\n\nAlso, should I verify